{"task_id": "Mbpp/630", "solution": "def get_coordinates(coordinate_tuple: tuple[int, int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts all 3x3 adjacent coordinates of the given coordinate tuple, including itself.\n    The resulting list is sorted first by x-component, then by y-component.\n    \"\"\"\n    x, y = coordinate_tuple\n    adjacent_coords = []\n\n    for i in range(x - 1, x + 2):\n        for j in range(y - 1, y + 2):\n            adjacent_coords.append([i, j])\n\n    # The explicit sorting from the design context: \"sorted first by i (x-component) and then by j (y-component).\"\n    # The nested loops naturally generate coordinates in this order, so explicit sort is not strictly necessary\n    # if `range` is used and appended sequentially. However, to adhere strictly to the \"Sort the resulting list\"\n    # sequence diagram step, it's good practice to include it.\n    adjacent_coords.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], coord[1]))\n    \n    return adjacent_coords"}
